WEBVTT

00:00:00.435 --> 00:00:02.213
Choose any positive whole number.

00:00:03.104 --> 00:00:05.088
If it is even, divide by two.

00:00:05.978 --> 00:00:08.774
If it is odd, multiply by three and add one.

00:00:09.663 --> 00:00:10.333
Repeat.

00:00:11.222 --> 00:00:13.774
Starting at six, the path reaches one.

00:00:14.663 --> 00:00:18.581
The Collatz conjecture says this happens for every positive starting number.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:20.462
A simple rule.

00:00:21.351 --> 00:00:22.614
A universal claim.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.660
A number can rise before it falls.

00:00:27.551 --> 00:00:31.610
Checking a huge collection of starts still leaves infinitely many unchecked.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.971
A proof must exclude every alternative: an endless trajectory that escapes to

00:00:36.984 --> 00:00:40.579
infinity, and a cycle other than four, two, one.

00:00:41.469 --> 00:00:44.613
A convincing pattern is not yet a universal argument.

00:00:46.518 --> 00:00:47.936
Why spend effort on this?

00:00:48.826 --> 00:00:53.234
Collatz tests how much we understand about deterministic arithmetic dynamics:

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.339
exact rules with complicated long term behavior.

00:00:57.229 --> 00:01:00.991
Progress connects number theory, probability, and the study of when

00:01:01.004 --> 00:01:02.280
algorithms terminate.

00:01:03.169 --> 00:01:05.888
Its value is in understanding and new methods.

00:01:06.777 --> 00:01:09.625
A practical payoff cannot be promised in advance.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.872
The problem is traditionally associated with Lothar Collatz.

00:01:15.762 --> 00:01:19.757
In the nineteen seventies, Riho Terras and, independently, C.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:20.041
J.

00:01:20.054 --> 00:01:24.023
Everett proved that almost every start eventually falls below itself, in

00:01:24.036 --> 00:01:25.041
natural density.

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:30.144
Daniel Bernstein and Jeffrey Lagarias developed a precise two adic coding of

00:01:30.157 --> 00:01:31.059
the dynamics.

00:01:31.948 --> 00:01:35.170
These are different advances toward understanding the same problem.

00:01:37.102 --> 00:01:40.980
Terence Tao made a major advance in twenty nineteen, published in twenty

00:01:40.993 --> 00:01:41.779
twenty two.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:45.930
Choose any bound that tends to infinity, however slowly.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:51.240
Almost every orbit eventually goes below that bound, in logarithmic density.

00:01:52.129 --> 00:01:54.577
This is much stronger control of orbit minima.

00:01:55.466 --> 00:01:57.941
But it does not say that every orbit reaches one.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.678
The exceptional starts remain the central difficulty.

00:02:03.602 --> 00:02:07.648
Our contribution is an evolving research map and a collection of internally

00:02:07.661 --> 00:02:08.756
reviewed arguments.

00:02:09.647 --> 00:02:13.513
The current snapshot has three hundred fifty six nodes and five hundred

00:02:13.526 --> 00:02:15.072
thirty two relationships.

00:02:15.961 --> 00:02:18.539
Green means checked within the stated assumptions.

00:02:19.428 --> 00:02:21.631
Unfilled nodes mark open obligations.

00:02:22.521 --> 00:02:25.820
The count of green nodes measures neither the percentage solved nor the

00:02:25.832 --> 00:02:27.250
likelihood of success.

00:02:28.139 --> 00:02:31.335
External review and novelty remain separate questions.

00:02:33.268 --> 00:02:37.263
In one restricted family, recent work separates a difficult denominator

00:02:37.276 --> 00:02:42.121
estimate into two sources: deep divisibility and large multiplicative orders.

00:02:43.012 --> 00:02:46.930
A further reduction compresses a first coefficient certificate to degree

00:02:46.942 --> 00:02:51.015
below four d, independent of the full matrix length, under stated

00:02:51.027 --> 00:02:51.904
assumptions.

00:02:52.793 --> 00:02:56.105
The needed bounds for the actual coefficients are still missing.

00:02:56.994 --> 00:03:01.027
Even closing this branch would not settle arbitrary Collatz trajectories.

00:03:02.935 --> 00:03:07.355
This map and explanation are by Khamit Kadyrbekov and Daniyal Kadirbekov,

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.762
with artificial intelligence assistance for exploration, drafting, and

00:03:11.775 --> 00:03:12.845
internal checking.

00:03:13.735 --> 00:03:17.988
Our workflow separates arithmetic derivation, structural reformulation,

00:03:18.143 --> 00:03:20.836
critical review, and connection to the full goal.

00:03:21.725 --> 00:03:24.895
Earlier mathematicians are credited for their published work.

00:03:25.784 --> 00:03:28.027
They are not collaborators on this project.

00:03:28.916 --> 00:03:31.867
Responsibility for the claims remains with the authors.

00:03:33.768 --> 00:03:37.402
One route to a full proof is to show that every odd start greater than one

00:03:37.415 --> 00:03:39.232
eventually falls below itself.

00:03:40.123 --> 00:03:42.649
Strong induction would then finish the argument.

00:03:43.538 --> 00:03:46.038
We have not established that universal descent.

00:03:46.927 --> 00:03:50.084
There is no defensible completion date or percentage remaining.

00:03:50.973 --> 00:03:55.432
Explore the map, inspect the assumptions, challenge a step, or help prove a

00:03:55.445 --> 00:03:57.133
clearly stated missing lemma.

00:03:58.022 --> 00:04:00.303
That is how this project can move forward.
